<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.4: Factoring Trinomials Using the “ac” Method and Substitution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01653</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.4: Factoring Trinomials Using the “ac” Method and Substitution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>dea32168-6d1d-4911-a2cc-c6e062a22031</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>Students have already learned one way to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\). In this activity, they will be introduced to the “\(ac\)” method, which works on any trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<para>Students will also learn to recognize situations where they can substitute the variable \(u\) for an expression, which leaves them a trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\), which is much more recognizable to factor.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Open the activity by introducing the “\(ac\)” method to students. Explain that this method will allow them to factor any trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\). Tell students that while this is a new way to factor trinomials, it is based on skills that they have already learned. Read through each step, stopping to address any confusion as needed.</para>
<para>Keep the steps visible to the class and work through the first problem. This will help solidify the process using the “\(ac\)” method. If students are feeling less confident in the process, allow them to work in pairs as they complete questions 2 – 3. This will encourage them to talk out the process as they learn a new method to factor trinomials.</para>
<para>As a class, read the text on factoring by substitution that precedes question 4. To provide a hint for question 4, display the equation \(u = y^2\). By using this equation to substitute, students will find a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) that they will recognize.</para>
<para>Allow time for students to complete questions 4 – 6. Pause for student questions about using substitution to factor before moving to the activity synthesis.</para>
<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>Let’s learn a new method that always works on any trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>

<note>
  <para> <emphasis> How to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using the “\(ac\)” method: </emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor any GCF. Don’t forget!</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the product \(ac\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
    </emphasis>Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
  <list>
    <item>multiply to \(ac\): \(m \cdot n = a \cdot c\).</item>
    <item>add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\).</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Split the middle term using \(m\) and \(n\).</para>
  <para>    \(ax^2 + mx + nx + c\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Factor by grouping.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
</note>

<para>Factor each trinomial using the “\(ac\)” method.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(16x^2 − 32x + 12\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(4(2x − 3)(2x − 1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(6y^2 + y − 15\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((3y + 5)(2y − 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(48z^3 − 102z^2 − 45z\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(3z(8z + 3)(2z − 5)\)</para>
<para>There will be times when a trinomial does not seem to be in the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<para>In some of these cases, we can make a substitution that allows the trinomial to be written in the \(ax^2 + bx + c\) form. This is called factoring by substitution.</para>
<para>It is standard to use \(u\) for the substitution. The substitution for \(u\) might be a variable with an exponent, such as \(x^2\), or it might be a binomial, such as \((x − 3)\).</para>
<para>Factor each trinomial using the substitution method.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(y^4 − y^2 − 20\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((y^2 + 4)(y^2 − 5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    (x−5)^2 + 6(x − 5) + 8\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x − 3)(x − 1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(x^4 − 3x^2 − 28\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x^2 − 7)(x^2 + 4)\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>In this activity, students learned two new ways to factor trinomials. By using the “\(ac\)” method, students can solve any trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<para>Ask the class as a whole to brainstorm the steps used in the “\(ac\)” method.</para>

<note>
  <para> <emphasis> How to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using the “\(ac\)” method: </emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para>Step 1 - 
    Factor any GCF. Don’t forget!</para>
  <para>Step 2 - 
    Find the product \(ac\).</para>
  <para>Step 3 - 
    Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
  <list>
    <item>multiply to \(ac\): \(m \cdot n = a \cdot c\).</item>
    <item>add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\).</item>
  </list>
  <para>Step 4 - 
    Split the middle term using \(m\) and \(n\).
    \(ax^2 + mx + nx + c\)</para>
  <para>Step 5 - 
    Factor by grouping.</para>
  <para>Step 6 - 
    Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
</note>

<para>By using substitution, some trinomials that could not previously be factored using the methods we know will now seem to have the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<para>Ask students to name sample values for which \(u\) could be substituted in a polynomial:</para>
<list>
  <item> \(x^2, y^2, (x − 7)\) </item>
</list>
<title> 6.5.4: Self Check</title>
<para>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption>
  QUESTION:
  <para>Factor using the “\(ac\)” method: \(6x^2 + 13x + 2\).</para>
  </caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis> Answers </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\((x + 2)(6x + 1)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Multiply using FOIL to check to make sure the answer is equivalent to the starting polynomial.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\((6x + 2)(x + 1)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: These factors do not multiply to the correct \(b\) term. The correct answer is \((x + 2)(6x + 1)\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\((x − 2)(6x − 1)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: These factors do not multiply to the correct \(b\) term. The correct answer is \((x + 2)(6x + 1)\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\((x − 2)(6x + 1)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: These factors do not multiply to the correct \(b\) or \(c\) term. The correct answer is \((x + 2)(6x + 1)\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>6.5.4: Additional Resources</title>
<para>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</para>
<title>Factoring Trinomials Using the “\(ac\)” Method</title>
<para>Another way to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) is the “\(ac\)” method. (The “\(ac\)” method is sometimes called the grouping method.) The “\(ac\)” method is actually an extension of the methods you used in the last section to factor trinomials with a leading coefficient of 1. This method is very structured (that is, step by step), and it always works!</para>
<para>Example 1</para>
<para>Factor using the “\(ac\)” method: \(6x^2 + 7x + 2\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor any GCF.</para>
<para>  Is there a greatest common factor?
  \(6x^2 + 7x + 2\)
  No!</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the product \(ac\).</para>
<para>  \(
  {\style{color:red}a}
  {\style{color:red}x}
  ^
  {\style{color:red}2}
  \;
  {\style{color:red}+}
  \;
  {\style{color:red}b}
  {\style{color:red}x}
  \;
  {\style{color:red}+}
  \;
  {\style{color:red}c}
  \\6x^2+7x+2\)
  \(6 \cdot 2\)
  12</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
<list>
  <item>multiply to \(ac\): \(m \cdot n = a \cdot c\).</item>
  <item>add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\).</item>
</list>
<para>Find two numbers that multiply to 12 and add to 7. Both factors must be positive.
  \(3 \cdot 4 = 12\)
  \(3 + 4 = 7\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Split the middle term using \(m\) and \(n\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aa741f0ec41115d2cf8f76221c37bf20a304e192"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Rewrite \(7x\) as \(3x + 4x\). It would give the same result if we used \(4x + 3x\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f95237074a7e4936a16ad8661e90cf711322646d"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Notice that \(6x^2 + 3x + 4x + 2\) is equal to \(6x^2 + 7x + 2\). We just split the middle term to get a more useful form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Factor by grouping.</para>
<para>  \(3x(2x + 1) + 2(2x + 1)\)
  \((2x + 1)(3x + 2)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
<para>  \((2x + 1)(3x + 2)\)
  \(6x^2 + 4x + 3x + 2\)
  \(6x^2 + 7x + 2 \checkmark\)
</para>
<para>The “\(ac\)” method is summarized here:</para>
<note>
  <para> <emphasis> How to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using the “\(ac\)” method: </emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
    </emphasis>Factor any GCF. Don’t forget!</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the product \(ac\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
  <list>
    <item>multiply to \(ac\): \(m \cdot n = a \cdot c\).</item>
    <item>add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\).</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Split the middle term using \(m\) and \(n\).</para>
  <para>    \(ax^2 + mx + nx + c\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Factor by grouping.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
</note>


<title>Try It: Factoring Trinomials Using the “\(ac\)” Method</title>
<para>Factor using the “\(ac\)” method: \(10y^2 − 55y + 70\).</para>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>Solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
<title> Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to use the “\(ac\)” method to factor a trinomial.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Is there a greatest common factor?</para>
<para>  \(10y^2-55y+70\)
  Yes. The GCF is 5.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Factor it.</para>
<para>  \(5(2y^2-11y+14)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
  </emphasis>The trinomial inside the parentheses has a leading coefficient that is not \(1\).</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{c}{\style{color:red}a}{\style{color:red}x}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:red}b}{\style{color:red}x}+{\style{color:red}c}\\5(2y^2-11y+14)\end{array}\) </para>
<para>Find the product \(ac\).
  \(ac=28\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Find two numbers that multiply to \(ac\) and add to \(b\).</para>
<para>  \((-4)(-7)=28\)
  \(-4+(-7)=-11\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - 
</emphasis>Split the middle term.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2bd20fbcb7c846a9536d0ae1464d5c3bde7ccaea"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Factor the trinomial by grouping.</para>
<para> \(5(y(2y-7)-2(2y-7))\)
  \(5(y-2)(2y-7)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying all three factors.</para>
<para>
  <m:math display="inline">
    <m:semantics>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:mrow>
          <m:mspace width="4em"/>
          <m:mtable>
            <m:mtr>
              <m:mtd columnalign="center">
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mtd>
            </m:mtr>
            <m:mtr>
